  "body": "Found during the work on #2394 (example case turning/cone-thread).\n\n## What happens\n\nA thread at the free end of a part cannot start in front of the end face. The threading verdict blocks the post.\n\n- The thread kernel puts a V at each lattice point whose band (centre plus and minus half the insert width) overlaps the window (`crates/motion/src/turning/thread.rs`, `k_hi`).\n- The meridian scan (`crates/motion/src/turning/revolve.rs`, `scan`) judges each interval that a band covers. In front of the end face the model radius is 0. The V floor is capped at the crest radius. The scan reads the difference as rest.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Model a bar with a 10 mm thread diameter, 0.92 mm deep V grooves each 1.5 mm, and the end face at Z78. The last groove centre is at Z77.5.\n2. Add a Threading operation with a 1.2 mm wide, 60 degree insert. Set the window top to Z78 or above.\n3. Generate.\n\nResult: `finish envelope near move 64: 0.0000 mm gouge, 5.0000 mm rest`. The post is blocked. The band of the last V (Z76.9 to Z78.1) is 0.1 mm past the end face.\n\nA window top of Z81 gives the same result: lattice points at Z79 and Z80.5 are in free air, and the verdict reads them as 5 mm of rest.\n\n## What is expected\n\nA threading pass can start in free air in front of the end face. This is the usual practice: the pass starts some pitches before the part so that the spindle and the feed are synchronised. Air in front of the end face is not rest.\n\n## Workaround\n\nPut the last groove of the model so that the insert band ends exactly on the end face. Each pass then starts inside the part length with a feed plunge (G32 output). The case of #2394 does this: last centre at Z77.4, band Z76.8 to Z78.0.\n\nThis workaround is not safe with the G76 cycle. See the related issue \"G76 cycle starts inside the part\".\n\nRefs #2394\n",
